- The group consensus firmly identifies 34... h6+ 35. Kf6 Ng4# as the shortest forced mating sequence, delivering checkmate in exactly two moves (three plies).
- Dissenting proposals favoring 34... Kg7 fail to guarantee the shortest mate because non-checking moves permit delaying defenses, whereas 34... h6+ strictly forces White's only legal response (35. Kf6) directly into checkmate.
- Under FIDE rules, pinned pieces fully retain their control over squares, confirming that White has no escape route to the e-file after 35... Ng4#.
- The definitive sequence to maintain remains: h6+ Kf6 Ng4#.
}

