The consensus remains decisively centered on the forced 3-ply checkmate sequence `h6+ Kf6 Ng4#`. Analysis conclusively shows that the alternative `34... Kg7` fails to force mate in two because moves like `35. Nh5+` vacate the f4-square, allowing the White king to escape via `36. Kf4`. In contrast, `34... h6+` leaves White with `35. Kf6` as the sole legal move, after which `35... Ng4#` delivers an airtight mate since the e-file remains legally impassable under FIDE Rule 3.9.2.
}

